From 8b6be966c45157ad9a436929ba28647b413ae57e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 02:21:43 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] fix(turning): read a lathe holder body on the holder's
 own side of the axis

The holder law (#369) read a body point at |X + x| with X signed along
the path's meridian, whose + side is the path's first off-axis point.
A rear tool starting a hair past centre put every later tip at X < 0 of
that frame, and its body was read mirrored through the axis - a lip
reaching the part judged clear. The holder's side is now a per-path
property (the side the path reaches farthest on, a past-centre overrun
of at most 2.5 mm allowed on the other); the body is read at -X on the
- side, and the drawn body flips its radial alike. A path on both sides
beyond that is refused by name. Generated paths (every tip at rho >= 0)
read byte-identically to before.

Fixes #1102

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
